course: everyday
lesson: 233
title: The sea, waves and swimming pools
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: The sea moves all the time. Waves can knock a child over and roll them along the bottom, and the sea floor can suddenly drop away, so beginners stay in shallow water close to a grown-up.
explain: One sign everyone near a coast should know: if the ground shakes and then the sea suddenly pulls back far from the shore, a tsunami is coming. Run to high ground or far inland at once, without waiting for a warning.
explain: Pools feel safe, but most child drownings happen in home pools when nobody is watching. Pool rules are about slipping, pushing, drains and never swimming without a grown-up watching.
explain: Example: a girl in Florida notices the gate of the pool fence at her aunt's house is open while her toddler cousin plays nearby. She shuts it and tells her aunt, and that small act could save a life.
fact: Take all your litter home from the beach, including bottle caps and small bits of plastic, because plastic in the sea kills turtles, birds and fish.
fact: If the sea suddenly pulls back far from the shore after an earthquake, a tsunami is coming, so run to high ground at once.
fact: When you first swim in the sea, stay in shallow water close to a grown-up, because the sea floor can drop suddenly.
fact: Never turn your back on the sea; with a grown-up near, turn sideways to a big wave or dive under it.
fact: If a wave knocks you down and you feel hurt or dizzy, get out of the water, rest and tell a grown-up.
fact: On a boat trip, wear a life jacket and stay in the area the crew tells you.
fact: Walk, never run, by a pool, because wet floors are slippery and falls onto tiles cause head injuries.
fact: Never push, dunk or hold anyone under water, even in fun, because they can hit the side, swallow water and drown.
fact: Keep hair, fingers and toys away from pool drains and outlets, because strong suction can hold you under.
fact: If you need the toilet while swimming, get out and go, and shower before getting back in.
fact: Never go in or near a pool without a grown-up watching; most child drownings happen in home pools.
fact: Close an open pool gate and tell a grown-up, because pool fences keep small children from falling in.
fact: Put sunscreen on again after swimming and drink water, because water washes sunscreen off and you lose water in the heat.
exercise: You are packing up after a day at the beach. What should you do with bottle caps and plastic bits? | Take them all home or put them in a bin | Bury them in the sand | Leave the tiny ones, as they do not matter | Throw them into the waves
exercise: Why is plastic litter on the beach so harmful? | It ends up in the sea and kills turtles, birds and fish | It soaks up the sunshine and makes the sand far too hot to walk on | It turns into seaweed | It only spoils the look of the beach for the people on holiday
exercise: After an earthquake, the sea suddenly pulls far back from the shore. What should you do? | Run to high ground or far inland at once | Walk out to look at the fish left behind | Wait on the beach for an official warning | Take photos of the empty sea floor
exercise: What does the sea pulling back suddenly after an earthquake warn of? | A tsunami is coming | The tide will be low all day | A big storm has just ended | The water is safe for paddling
exercise: You are swimming in the sea for the first time. Where should you stay? | In shallow water close to a grown-up | Out past the breaking waves, where it is calm and flat | Wherever your friends go | Over by the rocks, where it is deeper and more fun
exercise: A big wave is coming towards you. What should you NOT do? | Turn your back on it | Turn sideways to it | Dive under it with a grown-up near | Keep watching it come
exercise: On a boat trip, the crew says to stay on the lower deck. What should you do? | Wear your life jacket and stay where the crew says | Climb up onto the railing to get a better view of the sea | Go to the very front of the boat where the spray is | Take off the life jacket once the sea is calm
exercise: Your friend is running round the pool to get to the slide. What should you tell her? | Walk, because wet tiles are slippery | Run faster, because then she will slip less | Run on tiptoe so her toes grip better | Run, but only near the shallow end of the pool
exercise: Why should you never hold a friend under water, even as a joke? | They can swallow water and drown | It makes the water cloudy | Only bad swimmers are at risk | It ruins their swimming goggles
exercise: Your long hair is floating near the drain at the bottom of the pool. What is the danger? | Strong suction can pull it in and hold you under | The drain will get blocked with hair and the pool will overflow | The chlorine is stronger near the drain | Drains make the water much colder around that end
exercise: You need the toilet while swimming. What should you do? | Get out, go to the toilet and shower before going back | Go in the water, as the chlorine cleans it all straight away | Wait until the end of the afternoon | Go in the deep end of the pool, where nobody will notice it
exercise: Your friends want to swim in a holiday villa pool while the adults are out. What should you do? | Not go in without a grown-up watching | Swim, but only in the shallow end of the pool | Go in one at a time while the others watch | Swim, as long as everyone is wearing their goggles
exercise: You see the gate in the pool fence is open. What should you do? | Close it and tell a grown-up | Leave it so people can get in easily | Prop it open with a garden chair | Close it only if a toddler is near
exercise: You have been swimming outdoors all afternoon. What should you do? | Put sunscreen on again and drink water | Nothing, water keeps you safe | Only put sunscreen on your face | Wait until you feel burnt
exercise: Why do you need a drink at an outdoor pool even though you are wet all day? | You still lose water in the heat | Pool water is enough to drink | Being wet stops you getting thirsty | You only need to drink in winter
test: A boy in Hawaii feels a strong earthquake at the beach. A few minutes later the water rushes out, leaving fish flapping on the sand. What should he do? | Run to high ground straight away | Pick up the fish to help them | Wait for a siren before moving | Stay on the beach to see what happens
test: A girl in Cornwall turns round to wave at her mum while standing in the waves. A big wave knocks her down. What rule did she forget? | Never turn your back on the sea | Always swim far from the shore | Big waves only come in winter | Good swimmers never fall
test: Two boys are playing at the pool. One pushes the other in near the edge. What could go wrong? | He could hit the side and swallow water | He will just get a little bit wet, nothing more | Nothing, because pools are made to be safe for pushing | The water will splash the lifeguard's chair
test: Most young children who drown in the USA drown where? | In home pools when nobody is watching | In the open sea far from land | In rivers during floods | In large public pools with lifeguards
test: A toddler next door could reach the pool because the gate was propped open. Why do pools have fences? | To keep small children from falling in | To keep the autumn leaves out of the water | To stop the water evaporating in the hot sun | To keep the pool water warm when the wind blows
test: A girl's sunscreen says «water resistant» but she has been swimming for two hours. What should she do? | Put more on, as water washes it off | Nothing, it lasts all day once on | Only put it on her nose | Wait until she sees her skin turn red
test: Why should beginners not wade out until the water is at their chest? | The sea floor can drop suddenly and waves can knock them over | The water out there is always far too cold for beginners | Fish bite deeper in the sea | They will lose their sandals and have to walk home barefoot
test: A boy dives down to grab a toy stuck on the pool drain. Why is that risky? | The drain's suction can trap him under the water | The toy will get broken when he pulls it | Drains are always where the pool is at its shallowest | The lifeguard will make him leave the pool for the day
test: pool's slippery, race u to the slide!! | No running, walk by the pool | Yes, run fast so you don't slip | Run on tiptoes, it's safer | Only race near the deep end
test: just dunking my brother under for fun, he's fine | No, never hold anyone under water | Yes, if he's laughing he's fine | Yes, for a few seconds only, then let go | Only in the shallow end
test: need a wee but i'm swimming. just do it in the pool? | No, get out and go, shower before you get back | Yes, the chlorine in the water cleans it all up | Yes, if nobody sees | Only in the deep end where it's all spread out
test: sea just went way out after that shaking, can we look for shells | No, run to high ground now, a tsunami is coming | Yes, it's a great time for shells n stranded fish | Yes, but stay near the water | Wait n watch for the water to come back in first
explain: A boy in Florida dived into the shallow end of a pool; he hit his head on the bottom and hurt his neck badly.
explain: A toddler in California slipped through an open pool gate while the grown-ups were chatting; she was found in the water just in time and needed hospital care.
fact: Drowning is a leading cause of death for young children, and many drownings happen in home pools.
fact: Diving into shallow water causes serious neck and spine injuries every summer, so dive only where the water is deep and diving is allowed.
test: gonna dive into the shallow end | No, dive only where it's deep and allowed | Yes, if you go fast n flat | Yes, it's ok if u go in hands first | Only from the side
test: Why must the pool gate always be shut? | Small children can wander in and drown | It keeps the water clean and free of dust | It stops leaves blowing in | Gates are just for show and do nothing
test: big wave knocked me down, my ears hurt | Get out, rest and tell an adult | Go back in straight away to shake it off | Swim out further, past where the waves break | Dive under the next wave so it can't hit u
test: did u hear a kid dived into the shallow end and broke his neck and now he's in a wheelchair? real lesson? | Never dive into shallow water | Dive only with your eyes shut | Dive if you're a good swimmer | Shallow water is softer to land in
test: apparently a girl's hair got sucked into the pool drain and held her under. what's the true lesson? | Keep hair, fingers and toys away from pool drains | Pool drains only ever pull in grown-ups, never children | Tie your hair up and then it's fine anywhere in the pool | Drains only switch on at night when nobody is swimming
test: my mate says the sea went way out at his holiday beach and everyone ran down to look at the fish. right move? | No, it can mean a tsunami; run to high ground | Yes, it's a chance to collect shells | Yes, the sea won't come back for hours | Yes, if a grown-up is with you
exam: Identify the appropriate response where the sea suddenly withdraws far from the shore following an earthquake. | To run to high ground at once, as a tsunami approaches | To walk out across the sand to collect the stranded fish | To remain on the beach and watch | To swim out to meet the wave before it reaches the shore
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the holding of another person under water in play? | It may cause him to strike the side, swallow water and drown | It is entirely harmless provided it is done only briefly | It is acceptable in a shallow pool | It strengthens a person's swimming and his ability to hold his breath
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice upon finding a pool gate open. | It is closed and an adult is informed | It is left open for others | It is propped open with a chair | It is ignored if no child is near
exam: Ascertain the place where most drownings of young children occur. | In home swimming pools | In bathtubs at school | In public fountains | In deep oceans
